export const D1={title:'What epidemiology is and how the event works',hook:'In 1854, cholera killed more than 500 people in ten days in one London neighborhood. A physician named John Snow mapped the deaths, noticed they clustered around a single water pump on Broad Street and persuaded officials to remove its handle. He did not know what caused cholera, but he showed where it came from. That is epidemiology: finding patterns in populations so that disease can be prevented.',objectives:['Describe the HOSA Epidemiology event and its test plan.','Define epidemiology and its key terms: distribution, determinants and populations.','Describe landmark events in the history of epidemiology.','Explain the core functions of epidemiology and descriptive epidemiology by person, place and time.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Epidemiology is a **solo, written-test event**: **50 multiple-choice questions in 60 minutes**, plus **one essay question** that is judged only to **break ties**. HOSA provides a basic handheld calculator for addition, subtraction, multiplication, division and square roots; personal calculators are not allowed. The **test plan** is **Introduction to Epidemiology 30%** (basic concepts, types of studies, measuring health and disease, causation and surveillance), **World and Government Response to Infectious Diseases 20%** (WHO infectious diseases, CDC activities, government responses) and **CDC Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report 50%** (case studies, investigations, surveillance summaries and recommendations).',
'Official references are CDC’s **Public Health 101 Series**, **ActivEpi**, ATSDR’s **Environmental Medicine Education Resources** and the **MMWR**, with content posted as of **September 1, 2026**. Because half the test comes from MMWR, regular reading of recent reports is essential; this course teaches the concepts and reading skills you need to understand any report.',
{t:'table',caption:'Epidemiology test plan',head:['Area','Weight','Includes'],rows:[['Introduction to Epidemiology','30%','Concepts, study types, measures, causation, surveillance'],['World and Government Response','20%','WHO infectious diseases, CDC activities, government responses'],['CDC MMWR','50%','Case studies, investigations, surveillance summaries, recommendations']]}]],
['Defining epidemiology',[
'A widely used definition describes epidemiology as **the study of the distribution and determinants of health-related states or events in specified populations, and the application of this study to the control of health problems**. **Distribution** means the frequency and pattern of health events—how often they occur and among whom, where and when. **Determinants** are the causes and factors that influence whether health events occur: biological, behavioral, environmental and social. **Health-related states or events** include not only infectious diseases but also chronic diseases, injuries, birth defects, mental health and health behaviors.',
'Clinicians focus on the health of an **individual patient**; epidemiologists treat the **population** or community as their “patient.” Epidemiology is also an **applied** science: its purpose is to guide action—vaccination campaigns, food recalls, smoke-free laws and seat-belt policies all grew from epidemiologic evidence.',
match('Term and meaning',[['Distribution','Frequency and pattern by person, place and time'],['Determinants','Causes and factors that influence health'],['Specified population','The group being studied'],['Application','Using findings to control health problems'],['Health-related event','Disease, injury, behavior or other outcome'],['Population as patient','The epidemiologist’s focus']])]],
['Landmarks in the history of epidemiology',[
'**Hippocrates** (about 400 BCE) suggested that environment and behavior influence disease. **John Graunt** (1662) analyzed London’s **Bills of Mortality**, counting deaths by cause and showing patterns by age, sex and season. **William Farr** (1800s) built a system of vital statistics and is called a father of modern surveillance. **John Snow** (1854) linked cholera deaths to the **Broad Street pump** and compared death rates among households served by two water companies—an early natural experiment.',
'In the twentieth century, **Doll and Hill** linked cigarette smoking to lung cancer through case-control and cohort studies in the 1950s; the **Framingham Heart Study** (begun in 1948) identified risk factors for heart disease such as high blood pressure and cholesterol. The global **smallpox eradication** campaign, declared successful in **1980**, used surveillance and containment (ring vaccination). CDC’s **Epidemic Intelligence Service** (founded 1951) trains “disease detectives.”',
match('Person or study and contribution',[['John Graunt','Bills of Mortality, 1662'],['William Farr','Vital statistics and surveillance'],['John Snow','Broad Street pump and cholera'],['Doll and Hill','Smoking and lung cancer'],['Framingham Heart Study','Heart disease risk factors'],['Smallpox eradication','Declared in 1980']])]],
['Core functions of epidemiology',[
'Epidemiologists perform several **core functions**: **public health surveillance** (ongoing collection, analysis and interpretation of health data); **field investigation** (responding to outbreaks and unusual events); **analytic studies** (testing hypotheses about causes); **evaluation** (judging whether programs work); **linkages** (sharing data and collaborating with laboratories, clinicians and other agencies); and **policy development** (providing evidence for laws and guidelines).',
'Epidemiology contributes to public health by assessing the community’s health, supporting individual decisions (such as understanding personal risk), completing the clinical picture of a disease (its natural history and spectrum) and searching for causes. These functions match the **10 Essential Public Health Services**, which begin with assessing and monitoring population health.',
sort('Which core function?',['Surveillance','Field investigation','Evaluation'],[['Weekly review of reported measles cases',0],['Interviewing guests after a wedding outbreak',1],['Measuring whether a smoking-cessation program lowered smoking rates',2],['Tracking emergency-department visits for heat illness',0],['Collecting stool samples from ill restaurant patrons',1]])]],
['Descriptive epidemiology: person, place and time',[
'**Descriptive epidemiology** organizes data by **person** (age, sex, race and ethnicity, occupation, behaviors), **place** (residence, workplace, school, geography, urban or rural) and **time** (trends over years, seasons, days or hours, and the epidemic curve). It answers **who, where and when**, which helps generate **hypotheses** about **why and how**—the work of **analytic epidemiology**.',
'Descriptive data reveal patterns such as a seasonal rise in influenza each winter, higher rates of Lyme disease in the Northeast and upper Midwest or a cluster of illness among people who ate at one restaurant. Maps (like Snow’s), tables and graphs make these patterns visible. Always ask what the population at risk is: counting cases without knowing how many people could have been affected can mislead.',
sort('Person, place or time?',['Person','Place','Time'],[['Cases highest among adults over 65',0],['Cases cluster in one county',1],['Cases peak each January',2],['Most cases are health care workers',0],['Illness began 12–48 hours after a dinner',2],['Cases live near a contaminated well',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('A neighborhood cluster',{a:{text:'Health officials notice 30 cases of a gastrointestinal illness in one week in a town that usually reports 2. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the first descriptive question?',choices:[['Who became ill, where do they live or eat and when did symptoms start?','b','Correct. Person, place and time.',true],['Which drug company should make a treatment?','b','Describe the pattern first.',false]]},b:{text:'Most cases ate at the same café on Friday or Saturday. What kind of reasoning is this?',choices:[['Generating a hypothesis from descriptive data','c','Correct. Next, test it analytically.',true],['Proof that the café caused the illness','c','A pattern is not proof.',false]]},c:{text:'Investigators plan a study comparing ill and well café customers to test the hypothesis.',end:true}})]],
["Epidemiology careers and ethics",["Epidemiologists work in **local, state and federal health departments**, **hospitals** (infection prevention), **universities**, **pharmaceutical companies**, **international organizations** and the **military**. Most hold a **Master of Public Health (MPH)** or a doctoral degree; many begin in fellowships such as CDC’s **EIS** or the CSTE Applied Epidemiology Fellowship. Specialties include infectious disease, chronic disease, environmental, occupational, injury, maternal and child health, genetic and social epidemiology.","Epidemiology raises **ethical** questions. Investigators must protect **confidentiality** of health data, obtain **informed consent** for research, minimize harm and avoid **stigmatizing** groups when reporting results. Studies involving people are reviewed by **institutional review boards (IRBs)**; routine public health surveillance and outbreak response are often authorized by law instead. History reminds us why: the **Tuskegee Study** (1932–1972) withheld treatment from Black men with syphilis and led to modern research protections.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Setting and epidemiologist’s work","pairs":[["Hospital","Tracking health care–associated infections"],["State health department","Investigating outbreaks"],["University","Designing cohort studies"],["WHO","Global surveillance and guidelines"],["Pharmaceutical company","Drug safety and trials"],["IRB","Reviewing research ethics"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Solo 50-question test in 60 minutes with a tiebreaker essay; Introduction to Epidemiology 30%, World and Government Response 20%, MMWR 50%; basic calculator provided.','Epidemiology studies the distribution and determinants of health-related states in specified populations and applies the findings to control health problems; the population is the patient.','Landmarks: Graunt, Farr, Snow and the Broad Street pump, Doll and Hill, Framingham, smallpox eradication (1980); core functions include surveillance, field investigation, analytic studies and evaluation; descriptive epidemiology uses person, place and time.'],terms:[['Epidemiology','The study of the distribution and determinants of health events in populations and its application to control them.'],['Determinant','A factor that influences whether a health event occurs.'],['Descriptive epidemiology','Describing health events by person, place and time.']],mcqs:[q('What does distribution refer to in the definition of epidemiology?','The frequency and pattern of health events','The causes of disease','Drug delivery','Hospital locations','Who, where and when.'),q('Who linked cholera deaths to the Broad Street pump?','John Snow','William Farr','Louis Pasteur','Edward Jenner','1854 London.'),q('What percentage of the HOSA Epidemiology test comes from the MMWR?','50%','30%','20%','10%','Half the test.'),q('Which question does descriptive epidemiology answer?','Who, where and when','Only why','How to treat a patient','How much a drug costs','Person, place and time.'),q('The essay question in the Epidemiology event is used to:','Break ties','Replace the test','Count for 50%','Choose topics','Tiebreaker.'),q('Determinants are:','Factors that influence health events','Only death counts','Hospital budgets','Vaccines only','Causes and influences.'),q('Ongoing collection, analysis and interpretation of health data is:','Surveillance','Evaluation','Linkage','Policy development','Core function.'),q('Which study identified high blood pressure and cholesterol as heart disease risk factors?','Framingham Heart Study','Bills of Mortality','Broad Street study','Tuskegee study','Begun in 1948.')],frq:{prompt:'Define epidemiology, explain each key part of the definition and describe how John Snow’s cholera investigation illustrates descriptive and analytic epidemiology.',rubric:[['Definition',['distribution','determinants','health-related','specified populations','application','control']],['Explanation',['frequency','pattern','causes','factors','population','community']],['Snow’s investigation',['cholera','broad street','pump','map','water company','1854']],['Descriptive and analytic',['person','place','time','hypothesis','compare','rates','action','handle']]],model:'Epidemiology is the study of the distribution and determinants of health-related states or events in specified populations, and the application of this study to the control of health problems. Distribution means the frequency and pattern of health events—how often they occur and among whom, where and when—while determinants are the biological, behavioral, environmental and social factors that cause or influence them. Health-related states include infections, chronic diseases, injuries and behaviors, and the specified population is the group being studied, so the community rather than a single patient is the focus. The final part of the definition stresses application: findings must be used to prevent and control problems. John Snow’s 1854 investigation of cholera in London shows both branches. Descriptively, he mapped deaths by place and time and saw them cluster around the Broad Street pump, generating a hypothesis that contaminated water spread the disease. Analytically, he compared death rates among households supplied by two water companies, one drawing water from a sewage-polluted part of the Thames, and found far higher rates among its customers. He then applied the evidence by persuading officials to remove the pump handle, which is the action-oriented purpose of epidemiology.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_PRIN,PH101]};

export const D2={title:'Measuring health and disease: counts, rates and ratios',hook:'A city reports 500 cases of a disease and a small town reports 50. Which place has the bigger problem? Without knowing how many people live in each, you cannot tell. Epidemiologists turn counts into rates so that groups of different sizes can be compared fairly. These calculations appear throughout the test and in every MMWR report.',objectives:['Distinguish counts, ratios, proportions and rates.','Calculate and interpret incidence, attack rate and prevalence.','Calculate mortality measures, including case fatality rate and infant mortality rate.','Explain why rates are age-adjusted and how prevalence relates to incidence and duration.'],sections:[
['Counts, ratios, proportions and rates',[
'A **count** is the number of cases. A **ratio** compares two quantities (for example, males to females, 3:2). A **proportion** is a ratio in which the numerator is part of the denominator, often shown as a percentage (40 of 200 people = 20%). A **rate** measures how fast events occur in a population over a **specified time** and includes the population at risk in the denominator—for example, 7.5 new cases per 1,000 people per year.',
'Rates are expressed per a convenient multiplier—per 100 (percent), 1,000, 10,000 or 100,000—so that small numbers are easy to read. Always identify the **numerator** (events), the **denominator** (population at risk) and the **time period**. The denominator should include only people who could develop the outcome: for example, cervical cancer rates use women, not the whole population.',
match('Measure and example',[['Count','150 new cases'],['Ratio','3 males for every 2 females'],['Proportion','20% of employees ill'],['Rate','7.5 new cases per 1,000 per year'],['Numerator','Number of events'],['Denominator','Population at risk']])]],
['Incidence and attack rate',[
'**Incidence** measures **new** cases during a period. The **incidence proportion (risk)** is new cases divided by the population at risk at the start; the **incidence rate** divides new cases by the total **person-time** at risk. An **attack rate** is an incidence proportion used in outbreaks: the number ill divided by the number exposed during a short period. A **secondary attack rate** counts cases among contacts of primary cases.',
ex('Calculating incidence and attack rate','In an invented town of 20,000 people, 150 new cases of disease X occur in one year. At an invented picnic, 80 of 200 guests become ill.',['Incidence = 150 ÷ 20,000 = 0.0075','Multiply by 1,000: 7.5 per 1,000 people per year','Attack rate = 80 ÷ 200 = 0.40','Express as a percentage: 40%'],'Incidence is 7.5 per 1,000 per year; the attack rate is 40% (numbers invented for practice).'),
'Incidence tells you about **risk** and is used to study causes. A rising incidence suggests more people are being newly exposed or becoming susceptible.']],
['Prevalence',[
'**Prevalence** measures **all existing cases** (new and old) at a point in time (**point prevalence**) or during a period (**period prevalence**), divided by the total population. It tells you about the **burden** of disease and helps plan services such as clinics and medications. For example, if 600 people in the invented town of 20,000 are living with disease X on July 1, the point prevalence is **600 ÷ 20,000 = 3%**, or **30 per 1,000**.',
'Prevalence depends on both **incidence** and **duration**: **prevalence ≈ incidence × average duration** (for rare diseases in a steady state). A chronic disease with low incidence but long survival (such as diabetes) can have high prevalence. A new treatment that keeps people alive longer without curing them **increases** prevalence; a cure or a high death rate **decreases** it.',
sort('Effect on prevalence',['Increases prevalence','Decreases prevalence'],[['Treatment that prolongs life without cure',0],['Higher incidence',0],['A new cure',1],['Higher death rate',1],['People with the disease move into the area',0],['Shorter duration of illness',1]])]],
['Mortality measures',[
'The **crude death rate** is total deaths divided by the midyear population, usually per 1,000 or 100,000. A **cause-specific mortality rate** counts deaths from one cause. The **case fatality rate (CFR)**—actually a proportion—is deaths from a disease divided by people **with** that disease: if 4 of 80 cases die, the CFR is **5%**. CFR measures **severity**, not risk in the population. **Proportionate mortality** is the percentage of all deaths due to a specific cause.',
'The **infant mortality rate** is deaths before age 1 divided by live births in the same year, per 1,000 live births: 12 infant deaths among 2,000 live births = **6 per 1,000**. **Neonatal** mortality covers the first 28 days; **maternal mortality** is usually expressed per 100,000 live births. **Years of potential life lost (YPLL)** emphasize deaths at younger ages.',
ex('Case fatality and infant mortality','In an invented outbreak, 4 of 80 cases die. An invented county has 12 infant deaths and 2,000 live births in a year.',['CFR = 4 ÷ 80 = 0.05 = 5%','Infant mortality = 12 ÷ 2,000 = 0.006','Multiply by 1,000: 6 per 1,000 live births'],'CFR 5%; infant mortality rate 6 per 1,000 live births (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Comparing populations and age adjustment',[
'**Crude rates** can mislead when populations differ in **age structure**. A retirement community may have a higher crude death rate than a college town simply because its residents are older. **Age adjustment (standardization)** applies each population’s age-specific rates to a **standard population** so the rates can be compared fairly. **Specific rates** (age-specific, sex-specific) avoid this problem by comparing like with like.',
'When comparing two groups, report both the **difference** (rate A minus rate B) and the **ratio** (rate A divided by rate B). Counts alone never tell you which group is more affected, and a difference in rates describes a pattern without proving its cause.',
{t:'w',kind:'equitylab',title:'Compare rates in two invented populations'}]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Two counties',{a:{text:'County A reports 300 flu hospitalizations among 150,000 residents; County B reports 120 among 40,000. (Numbers invented for practice.) Which has the higher rate?',choices:[['County B: 300 per 100,000 versus 200 per 100,000 in A','b','Correct. 120 ÷ 40,000 and 300 ÷ 150,000.',true],['County A, because it has more cases','b','Counts ignore population size.',false]]},b:{text:'County B has many more residents over 75. What should you do before concluding B is worse?',choices:[['Compare age-specific or age-adjusted rates','c','Correct.',true],['Nothing; crude rates are always fair','c','Age structure can confound.',false]]},c:{text:'After age adjustment, the rates are similar, so age structure explained much of the difference.',end:true}})]],
["Rates in MMWR reports",["MMWR reports usually present rates **per 100,000 population** for diseases and deaths, **per 1,000 live births** for infant deaths and **percentages** for survey results such as vaccination coverage. When reading a rate, ask three questions: **Of whom?** (the denominator), **Of what?** (the event) and **When?** (the time period). A rate of 12.4 per 100,000 per year means about 12 events for every 100,000 people at risk in one year.","Reports often compare rates with a **rate ratio** (rate in group A ÷ rate in group B) or a **rate difference**, and they may report **age-adjusted** rates to compare states or years. Watch for small denominators: a rate in a county of 2,000 people can change dramatically with one or two cases, so reports may suppress unstable rates.",{"t":"check","q":"In an invented county of 80,000 people, 20 deaths from a cause occur in a year. What is the rate per 100,000?","options":["25 per 100,000","2.5 per 100,000","250 per 100,000","20 per 100,000"],"why":"20 ÷ 80,000 × 100,000 = 25."}]]
],takeaways:['Count = number; ratio compares two numbers; proportion = part of the whole; rate includes time and the population at risk; identify numerator, denominator and time.','Incidence = new cases ÷ population at risk (attack rate in outbreaks); prevalence = all existing cases ÷ population; prevalence ≈ incidence × duration.','Case fatality rate = deaths ÷ cases (severity); infant mortality = infant deaths ÷ live births × 1,000; use age-specific or age-adjusted rates to compare populations with different age structures.'],terms:[['Incidence','The occurrence of new cases in a population at risk during a period.'],['Prevalence','The proportion of a population with a condition at a given time.'],['Case fatality rate','The proportion of people with a disease who die of it.']],mcqs:[q('Which measure counts only new cases?','Incidence','Prevalence','Proportionate mortality','Ratio','Prevalence includes old cases.'),q('In an invented outbreak, 30 of 150 attendees became ill. What is the attack rate?','20%','30%','5%','50%','30 ÷ 150.'),q('Prevalence is approximately equal to:','Incidence × duration','Incidence ÷ duration','Deaths ÷ cases','Births ÷ deaths','Steady state.'),q('A treatment that prolongs life without curing a disease will:','Increase prevalence','Decrease prevalence','Not change prevalence','Decrease incidence','People live longer with it.'),q('Case fatality rate measures:','Severity of a disease','Risk in the whole population','Number of births','Prevalence','Deaths among cases.'),q('Infant mortality rate uses which denominator?','Live births','Total population','All deaths','Number of mothers','Per 1,000 live births.'),q('Why are rates age-adjusted?','To compare populations with different age structures','To make numbers larger','To remove all bias','To count births','Standard population.'),q('In an invented town of 50,000, 250 new cases occur in a year. What is the incidence per 1,000?','5 per 1,000','50 per 1,000','0.5 per 1,000','250 per 1,000','250 ÷ 50,000 × 1,000.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the difference between incidence and prevalence, describe how they are related, and calculate both using invented data of your own.',rubric:[['Incidence',['new cases','population at risk','time','risk','attack rate']],['Prevalence',['existing','all cases','point','period','burden']],['Relationship',['incidence','duration','prevalence','increase','decrease','cure','death']],['Calculation',['divide','per 1,000','per 100,000','%','invented']]],model:'Incidence measures new cases of a condition that develop in a population at risk during a specified period, so it reflects the risk of getting the disease and is useful for studying causes; an attack rate is an incidence proportion used during outbreaks. Prevalence measures all existing cases—both new and old—at a point in time or over a period, divided by the total population, so it reflects the burden of disease and is useful for planning services. The two are linked by duration: for a stable, relatively rare condition, prevalence is approximately incidence multiplied by the average duration of illness. That means a long-lasting chronic disease can have high prevalence even if incidence is low, and a treatment that prolongs survival without curing the disease raises prevalence, while a cure or high death rate lowers it. Using invented data, suppose a town of 40,000 people has 200 new cases of a disease during 2026. The incidence is 200 ÷ 40,000 = 0.005, or 5 per 1,000 people per year. If 1,000 residents are living with the disease on July 1, the point prevalence is 1,000 ÷ 40,000 = 0.025, or 25 per 1,000 (2.5%). Because prevalence is five times the incidence, the average duration of the disease is roughly five years.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_PRIN,PH101]};

export const D3={title:'Study designs and measures of association',hook:'Does a new vaccine prevent illness? Does living near a highway raise asthma risk? Epidemiologists answer such questions with study designs that range from quick surveys to large randomized trials. Each design has strengths and limits, and each produces its own measure of association. Recognizing the design is one of the most tested skills in epidemiology.',objectives:['Distinguish descriptive and analytic, observational and experimental studies.','Describe cross-sectional, case-control, cohort, ecological and experimental designs.','Construct a 2 × 2 table and calculate risk ratio, odds ratio and attributable risk.','Choose the appropriate design for a research question.'],sections:[
['Descriptive and analytic studies',[
'**Descriptive studies** describe health events by person, place and time and generate hypotheses; they include **case reports**, **case series** and **descriptive surveys**. **Analytic studies** use a **comparison group** to test hypotheses about exposures and outcomes. Analytic studies are either **observational**—the investigator observes exposures that people chose or experienced—or **experimental**—the investigator **assigns** the exposure, such as a vaccine or a placebo.',
'An **ecological study** compares groups (such as countries or counties) rather than individuals—for example, average salt intake and stroke rates by country. Ecological studies are quick and cheap but risk the **ecological fallacy**: assuming that a relationship seen in groups holds for individuals.',
sort('Descriptive or analytic?',['Descriptive','Analytic'],[['A case report of an unusual infection',0],['Comparing lung cancer rates in smokers and nonsmokers',1],['A case series of 12 patients with a new rash',0],['A randomized trial of a new vaccine',1],['Comparing ill and well wedding guests on what they ate',1]])]],
['Cross-sectional studies',[
'A **cross-sectional study** measures exposure and outcome **at the same time** in a sample of a population—like a **snapshot** or **survey**. National surveys such as the **Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS)** and the **Youth Risk Behavior Survey (YRBS)** are cross-sectional. They measure **prevalence** and associations quickly and cheaply.',
'Because exposure and outcome are measured together, cross-sectional studies usually cannot show **which came first** (temporality). For example, a survey might find that people with back pain exercise less—but does inactivity cause back pain, or does pain cause inactivity? The measure is often a **prevalence ratio** or **prevalence odds ratio**.',
chk('Which type of epidemiological study is best characterized as a survey?',['Cross-sectional','Case-control','Cohort','Relational'],'HOSA sample question. A cross-sectional study is a snapshot survey.')]],
['Cohort studies',[
'A **cohort study** starts with people **classified by exposure** (exposed and unexposed) who do not yet have the outcome and follows them over time to compare **incidence**. **Prospective** cohorts follow people forward from now; **retrospective (historical)** cohorts use past records to identify exposure and then look at outcomes that have already occurred. Cohort studies establish **temporality**, can study **multiple outcomes** of one exposure and calculate **risk** directly.',
'Their measure of association is the **risk ratio (relative risk, RR)**: risk in the exposed ÷ risk in the unexposed. An RR of **1** means no association; **greater than 1** means increased risk; **less than 1** means a protective effect. Cohort studies can be expensive and slow for rare diseases or long latency periods, and participants may be lost to follow-up. Outbreak investigations at a defined event (a wedding, a cruise) often use a **retrospective cohort** of all attendees.',
match('Design and feature',[['Cohort','Starts with exposure, follows for outcome'],['Case-control','Starts with outcome, looks back at exposure'],['Cross-sectional','Exposure and outcome at the same time'],['Ecological','Groups, not individuals'],['Randomized controlled trial','Investigator assigns exposure'],['Retrospective cohort','Uses past records of exposure']])]],
['Case-control studies',[
'A **case-control study** starts with the **outcome**: it compares people **with** the disease (**cases**) to similar people **without** it (**controls**) and looks back at past exposures. It is efficient for **rare diseases** and diseases with long latency and can study **multiple exposures**. Because you choose the number of cases and controls, you cannot calculate risk directly; instead, you calculate the **odds ratio (OR)**, which approximates the risk ratio when the disease is rare.',
'Case-control studies are vulnerable to **recall bias** (cases may remember exposures differently than controls) and **selection bias** (controls that do not represent the source population). Good controls come from the same population as the cases. Doll and Hill’s early smoking studies used a case-control design.',
{t:'table',caption:'The 2 × 2 table',head:['','Ill (outcome +)','Well (outcome −)','Total'],rows:[['Exposed','a','b','a + b'],['Unexposed','c','d','c + d'],['Formula','Risk ratio = [a/(a+b)] ÷ [c/(c+d)]','Odds ratio = (a × d) ÷ (b × c)','']]}]],
['Calculating measures of association',[
ex('Risk ratio, odds ratio and attributable risk','In an invented outbreak, 40 of 100 people who ate the chicken salad became ill (60 did not); 10 of 100 who did not eat it became ill (90 did not).',['Risk in exposed = 40 ÷ 100 = 0.40','Risk in unexposed = 10 ÷ 100 = 0.10','Risk ratio = 0.40 ÷ 0.10 = 4.0','Odds ratio = (40 × 90) ÷ (60 × 10) = 3,600 ÷ 600 = 6.0','Attributable risk (risk difference) = 0.40 − 0.10 = 0.30','Attributable proportion among the exposed = 0.30 ÷ 0.40 = 75%'],'People who ate the salad had 4 times the risk of illness; 75% of illness among them can be credited to the salad (numbers invented for practice).'),
'**Attributable risk** (risk difference) is the excess risk in the exposed group that can be credited to the exposure, and the **attributable proportion** expresses that excess as a percentage of the risk in the exposed. The **population attributable risk** estimates how much disease in the whole population would be prevented if the exposure were removed. Report measures with a **95% confidence interval**: if the interval for an RR or OR includes **1**, the association is not statistically significant.']],
['Experimental studies and choosing a design',[
'In a **randomized controlled trial (RCT)**, investigators **randomly assign** participants to an intervention or a control (often a **placebo**), which balances known and unknown confounders. **Blinding** (single, double) prevents participants or investigators from knowing assignments. RCTs provide the strongest evidence for causation but can be expensive and are unethical for harmful exposures. **Community trials** assign interventions to whole communities, such as water fluoridation.',
'Choose the design to fit the question: a **cross-sectional** survey to measure prevalence; a **case-control** study for a rare disease or a quick outbreak investigation; a **cohort** study to follow an exposure over time; an **RCT** to test a treatment or vaccine.',
sort('Best design',['Case-control','Cohort','Randomized trial'],[['A rare cancer with many possible exposures',0],['Following nurses for 20 years to study diet and heart disease',1],['Testing whether a new vaccine prevents flu',2],['A quick study of what ill and well diners ate',0],['Comparing outcomes in workers exposed and not exposed to a solvent',1]])]]
],takeaways:['Descriptive studies generate hypotheses; analytic studies test them with a comparison group; observational studies observe exposures, experimental studies assign them; ecological studies risk the ecological fallacy.','Cross-sectional = survey snapshot (prevalence, no temporality); cohort starts with exposure and calculates risk ratio; case-control starts with disease and calculates odds ratio, efficient for rare diseases but prone to recall bias.','Risk ratio = risk exposed ÷ risk unexposed; odds ratio = ad ÷ bc; attributable risk is the proportion of disease in exposed individuals credited to the exposure; an RR or OR of 1 means no association; RCTs give the strongest evidence.'],terms:[['Cohort study','A study that follows exposed and unexposed groups over time to compare incidence.'],['Odds ratio','The odds of exposure among cases divided by the odds among controls.'],['Attributable risk','The disease risk in exposed people that can be credited to the exposure.']],mcqs:[q('What is the term for the proportion of a disease or other outcome in exposed individuals that can be credited to the exposure?','Attributable risk','Risk benefit','Cohort study','Odds advantage','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which type of epidemiological study is best characterized as a survey?','Cross-sectional','Case-control','Cohort','Relational','HOSA sample question.'),q('A study that starts with people who have a disease and compares their past exposures with people who do not is a:','Case-control study','Cohort study','Randomized trial','Ecological study','Calculates odds ratio.'),q('Which measure is calculated in a cohort study?','Risk ratio','Odds ratio only','Prevalence only','Proportionate mortality','Risk in exposed ÷ unexposed.'),q('In an invented study, risk is 0.30 in the exposed and 0.10 in the unexposed. What is the risk ratio?','3.0','0.20','0.33','30','0.30 ÷ 0.10.'),q('A risk ratio of 1.0 means:','No association','Strong harmful association','Protective effect','The study failed','Equal risk.'),q('Which bias especially threatens case-control studies?','Recall bias','Loss to follow-up','Randomization bias','Lead-time bias only','Cases remember differently.'),q('Which design gives the strongest evidence that a treatment works?','Randomized controlled trial','Case report','Ecological study','Cross-sectional survey','Random assignment.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare cohort and case-control studies, including how each selects participants, its measure of association and one strength and one limitation of each. Use an invented 2 × 2 table to calculate a risk ratio.',rubric:[['Cohort',['exposure','follow','incidence','risk ratio','temporality','expensive','loss to follow-up']],['Case-control',['cases','controls','disease','look back','odds ratio','rare','recall bias']],['Calculation',['2 × 2','a','b','c','d','risk','divide','invented']],['Interpretation',['1','greater than','times','association','confidence interval']]],model:'A cohort study begins with people who do not yet have the outcome, classifies them by exposure and follows them over time to compare the incidence of disease, so its measure of association is the risk ratio. A strength is that it establishes temporality—the exposure clearly comes before the outcome—and can examine several outcomes of one exposure; a limitation is that it can be slow and expensive, especially for rare diseases, and participants may be lost to follow-up. A case-control study begins with people who have the disease (cases) and similar people who do not (controls) and looks back to compare their past exposures, so it calculates an odds ratio, which approximates the risk ratio for rare diseases. A strength is that it is quick and efficient for rare diseases and many exposures; a limitation is recall bias, because cases may remember exposures differently than controls. Using an invented retrospective cohort from a banquet, 30 of 60 people who ate the custard became ill and 5 of 50 who did not eat it became ill. The risk in the exposed is 30 ÷ 60 = 0.50 and the risk in the unexposed is 5 ÷ 50 = 0.10, giving a risk ratio of 0.50 ÷ 0.10 = 5.0. People who ate the custard had five times the risk of illness, a strong association that would be supported if its 95% confidence interval did not include 1.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_PRIN,OS_STATS]};

export const D4={title:'Causation, bias and screening',hook:'An association is not the same as a cause. Ice cream sales and drownings both rise in summer, but ice cream does not cause drowning—hot weather drives both. Epidemiologists use models of causation, criteria for judging evidence and an understanding of bias and chance to decide when an exposure truly causes disease. They also measure how well screening tests find disease.',objectives:['Describe the epidemiologic triad, the chain of infection and the natural history of disease.','Apply the Bradford Hill criteria to judge causation.','Identify chance, bias and confounding as alternative explanations.','Calculate and interpret sensitivity, specificity and predictive values.'],sections:[
['Models of disease causation',[
'The **epidemiologic triad** describes infectious disease as the interaction of an **agent** (the microorganism or other cause), a **host** (the person, with characteristics such as age, immunity and behavior) and the **environment** (physical, biological and social conditions that bring agent and host together). Some versions add **time** and a **vector** (such as a mosquito or tick). Chronic diseases are often described with a **web of causation**, in which many interrelated factors contribute.',
'The **chain of infection** links six elements: **infectious agent**, **reservoir** (where the agent lives—people, animals or the environment), **portal of exit**, **mode of transmission** (direct contact, droplet, airborne, vehicle such as food or water, or vector), **portal of entry** and **susceptible host**. Breaking any link—hand hygiene, vaccination, mosquito control, water treatment—stops transmission.',
order('Chain of infection',['Infectious agent','Reservoir','Portal of exit','Mode of transmission','Portal of entry','Susceptible host'])]],
['Natural history and levels of prevention',[
'The **natural history of disease** progresses from the **stage of susceptibility** (exposure has not yet caused disease), to **subclinical disease** (changes are present but no symptoms—the **incubation period** for infections or **latency period** for chronic diseases), to **clinical disease** (signs and symptoms) and finally to **recovery, disability or death**. Not every infection causes symptoms; the **spectrum of disease** ranges from inapparent to fatal.',
'**Primary prevention** stops disease before it starts (vaccination, seat belts, smoking prevention). **Secondary prevention** detects disease early, during the subclinical stage, to improve outcomes (screening mammograms, blood pressure checks). **Tertiary prevention** reduces complications and disability after disease is established (cardiac rehabilitation, diabetic foot care).',
sort('Level of prevention',['Primary','Secondary','Tertiary'],[['Measles vaccination',0],['Colon cancer screening colonoscopy',1],['Physical therapy after a stroke',2],['Helmet laws',0],['Blood pressure screening at a health fair',1],['Diabetes education to prevent amputations',2]])]],
['Judging causation: the Bradford Hill criteria',[
'In 1965, Sir Austin Bradford Hill proposed considerations for judging whether an association is causal: **strength** (a large RR is less likely to be due to bias), **consistency** (repeated in different studies and populations), **specificity** (one cause leads to one effect—least important), **temporality** (the cause must come **before** the effect—the only **essential** criterion), **biological gradient** (dose–response: more exposure, more disease), **plausibility** (makes biological sense), **coherence** (fits with known facts), **experiment** (removing the exposure reduces disease) and **analogy** (similar causes produce similar effects).',
'No single study proves causation. Evidence that smoking causes lung cancer met almost every criterion: strong and consistent associations, dose–response, temporality, plausibility from laboratory studies and declining risk after quitting.',
match('Criterion and example',[['Temporality','Smoking began years before cancer'],['Strength','Smokers had 10 times the risk'],['Biological gradient','More cigarettes, more risk'],['Consistency','Same finding in many countries'],['Experiment','Risk falls after quitting'],['Plausibility','Carcinogens found in smoke']])]],
['Chance, bias and confounding',[
'Before accepting an association as causal, rule out three alternative explanations. **Chance** (random error) is assessed with **p-values** and **confidence intervals**; larger samples reduce random error. **Bias** (systematic error) includes **selection bias** (study participants differ from the population in ways related to exposure and outcome), **information bias** (errors in measuring exposure or outcome, including **recall bias** and **interviewer bias**) and **loss to follow-up**.',
'**Confounding** occurs when a third factor is associated with both the exposure and the outcome and distorts their relationship—for example, age confounding a link between gray hair and heart disease, or hot weather confounding ice cream and drowning. Confounding is controlled by **randomization**, **restriction**, **matching**, **stratification** and **statistical adjustment**.',
sort('Chance, bias or confounding?',['Chance','Bias','Confounding'],[['A small study finds a weak association with a wide confidence interval',0],['Cases remember their diets more carefully than controls',1],['Coffee drinkers smoke more, and smoking causes the disease',2],['Volunteers are healthier than the general population',1],['Age explains a link between retirement and heart disease',2]])]],
['Screening tests',[
'Screening tests are judged with a **2 × 2 table** comparing the test result to true disease status. **Sensitivity** = true positives ÷ all who have the disease (the ability to **detect** disease); **specificity** = true negatives ÷ all who do not have the disease (the ability to **rule out** disease). **Positive predictive value (PPV)** = true positives ÷ all positive tests; **negative predictive value (NPV)** = true negatives ÷ all negative tests. Predictive values depend on **prevalence**: when a disease is rare, even a good test has a low PPV.',
ex('Evaluating a screening test','An invented test is given to 1,000 people; 100 have the disease. The test is positive in 90 people with the disease and 45 people without it.',['True positives = 90; false negatives = 100 − 90 = 10','False positives = 45; true negatives = 900 − 45 = 855','Sensitivity = 90 ÷ 100 = 90%','Specificity = 855 ÷ 900 = 95%','PPV = 90 ÷ (90 + 45) = 90 ÷ 135 ≈ 66.7%','NPV = 855 ÷ (855 + 10) = 855 ÷ 865 ≈ 98.8%'],'Sensitivity 90%, specificity 95%, PPV about 67%, NPV about 99% (numbers invented for practice).'),
'A highly **sensitive** test is good for ruling out disease when negative; a highly **specific** test is good for confirming disease when positive. Screening programs work best for common, serious diseases with a detectable early stage and effective early treatment.']],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Coffee and disease',{a:{text:'An invented study finds coffee drinkers have twice the risk of a lung disease. Coffee drinkers in the study also smoke more. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the most likely explanation to check?',choices:[['Confounding by smoking','b','Correct. Smoking is linked to both coffee and disease.',true],['Coffee definitely causes the disease','b','Rule out confounding first.',false]]},b:{text:'After stratifying by smoking status, the risk ratio in each stratum is about 1.0. What do you conclude?',choices:[['The association was due to confounding','c','Correct.',true],['Coffee is protective','c','An RR of 1 means no association.',false]]},c:{text:'You report that smoking, not coffee, explains the excess risk.',end:true}})]],
["Screening programs and their pitfalls",["Good **screening programs** target diseases that are **common and serious**, have a **detectable preclinical phase** and an **effective early treatment**, and use tests that are **safe, acceptable and affordable**. Examples include newborn screening, blood pressure checks, cervical cancer screening and colorectal cancer screening. Programs must also provide follow-up for people who test positive.","Screening studies can be misleading. **Lead-time bias** makes survival look longer simply because disease was found earlier, even if death occurs at the same time. **Length bias** occurs because screening finds more slow-growing cases, which have better outcomes. **Overdiagnosis** finds conditions that would never have caused harm. Randomized trials that compare **mortality**, not just survival time, avoid these biases.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Screening term and meaning","pairs":[["Lead-time bias","Earlier detection appears to lengthen survival"],["Length bias","Screening finds slow-growing disease"],["Overdiagnosis","Finding disease that would never cause harm"],["False positive","Positive test in a person without disease"],["False negative","Negative test in a person with disease"],["Preclinical phase","Detectable before symptoms"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Epidemiologic triad: agent, host, environment; chain of infection: agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry, susceptible host; primary, secondary and tertiary prevention.','Bradford Hill criteria: strength, consistency, specificity, temporality (essential), biological gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, analogy; rule out chance, bias (selection, information, recall) and confounding.','Sensitivity = TP ÷ (TP + FN); specificity = TN ÷ (TN + FP); PPV = TP ÷ all positives; NPV = TN ÷ all negatives; predictive values depend on prevalence.'],terms:[['Confounding','Distortion of an association by a third factor linked to both exposure and outcome.'],['Sensitivity','The proportion of people with a disease who test positive.'],['Temporality','The requirement that a cause precede its effect.']],mcqs:[q('Which Bradford Hill criterion is essential for causation?','Temporality','Specificity','Analogy','Coherence','Cause before effect.'),q('A vaccine is an example of:','Primary prevention','Secondary prevention','Tertiary prevention','Screening','Prevents disease from starting.'),q('A mosquito that carries malaria is a:','Vector','Reservoir only','Host only','Portal of exit','Mode of transmission.'),q('Sensitivity is:','True positives ÷ all people with the disease','True negatives ÷ all without disease','True positives ÷ all positive tests','False positives ÷ all tests','Ability to detect disease.'),q('As prevalence decreases, the positive predictive value of a test:','Decreases','Increases','Stays the same','Becomes 100%','More false positives relative to true positives.'),q('Cases remembering exposures differently than controls is:','Recall bias','Confounding','Chance','Randomization','Information bias.'),q('Dose–response is also called:','Biological gradient','Consistency','Coherence','Analogy','More exposure, more disease.'),q('Which method controls confounding in a trial?','Randomization','Increasing recall','Using only cases','Ignoring age','Balances confounders.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how an epidemiologist decides whether an observed association between an exposure and a disease is causal, including alternative explanations and at least four Bradford Hill criteria.',rubric:[['Alternative explanations',['chance','p-value','confidence interval','bias','selection','information','recall']],['Confounding',['confounding','third factor','associated','both','stratification','randomization','adjustment']],['Bradford Hill',['strength','consistency','temporality','biological gradient','dose','plausibility','coherence','experiment']],['Conclusion',['no single study','weight of evidence','smoking','lung cancer','causal']]],model:'An epidemiologist first asks whether the association could be explained by something other than cause and effect. Chance is assessed with p-values and confidence intervals; a small study with a wide interval that includes 1 may reflect random error. Bias is systematic error, such as selection bias when participants are not representative or information bias like recall bias when cases remember exposures differently than controls. Confounding occurs when a third factor is associated with both exposure and outcome—for example, smoking confounding a link between coffee and lung disease—and is controlled with randomization, restriction, matching, stratification or statistical adjustment. If the association remains after these are addressed, the Bradford Hill criteria help judge causality. Temporality is essential: the exposure must come before the disease. Strength matters because a large risk ratio is less likely to be due to bias; consistency means the finding is repeated in different studies and populations; a biological gradient, or dose–response relationship, shows that more exposure brings more disease; plausibility and coherence mean it makes biological sense and fits known facts; and experiment shows that removing the exposure reduces disease. No single study proves causation; the weight of evidence across studies decides, as with smoking and lung cancer, which met nearly all of the criteria.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_PRIN,PH101]};

export const D5={title:'Public health surveillance and outbreak investigation',hook:'Somewhere in the United States every day, a laboratory reports a positive test for Salmonella, a clinician reports a case of measles and a health department notices more emergency visits than usual for vomiting. Surveillance turns these signals into information, and outbreak investigations turn information into action. MMWR reports describe both, so you need to know how they work.',objectives:['Define public health surveillance and describe types of surveillance systems.','Describe levels of disease occurrence and epidemic curve patterns.','List the steps of an outbreak investigation.','Write and apply a case definition.'],sections:[
['What surveillance is',[
'**Public health surveillance** is the **ongoing, systematic collection, analysis, interpretation and dissemination** of health data for planning, implementing and evaluating public health action. It is often summarized as “**information for action**.” Surveillance detects outbreaks, monitors trends, identifies high-risk groups, evaluates prevention programs and guides resources.',
'Types include **passive surveillance** (health care providers and laboratories report cases on their own—cheap but incomplete), **active surveillance** (health departments regularly contact providers to find cases—more complete but costly), **sentinel surveillance** (selected sites report data, such as influenza-like illness from certain clinics), **syndromic surveillance** (monitoring symptoms or emergency department chief complaints in near real time, such as CDC’s **National Syndromic Surveillance Program**) and **laboratory-based surveillance** (such as **PulseNet**, which compares bacterial DNA fingerprints to link cases across states).',
match('Surveillance type and example',[['Passive','Doctors report cases on their own'],['Active','Health department calls hospitals weekly'],['Sentinel','Selected clinics report flu-like illness'],['Syndromic','Emergency department chief complaints'],['Laboratory-based','PulseNet DNA fingerprinting'],['Notifiable disease reporting','Legally required case reports']])]],
['Notifiable diseases and the NNDSS',[
'Each state decides by law which diseases are **reportable**, and states voluntarily share data with CDC through the **National Notifiable Diseases Surveillance System (NNDSS)**. The **Council of State and Territorial Epidemiologists (CSTE)** and CDC agree on the list of nationally notifiable conditions and standard **surveillance case definitions**. Examples include measles, tuberculosis, salmonellosis, Lyme disease, hepatitis, pertussis and rabies.',
'Other national systems include the **National Vital Statistics System** (births and deaths), **BRFSS** (adult health behaviors by telephone survey), **YRBSS** (youth risk behaviors), **NHANES** (interviews plus physical exams and lab tests), the **National Healthcare Safety Network** (health care–associated infections) and **VAERS** (vaccine adverse event reports, run with FDA). Surveillance data have limits: underreporting, delays, changing case definitions and differences in testing.',
match('System and data',[['NNDSS','Nationally notifiable disease reports'],['NVSS','Births and deaths'],['BRFSS','Adult behaviors by phone survey'],['YRBSS','High school students’ risk behaviors'],['NHANES','Interviews plus physical exams'],['VAERS','Reports of possible vaccine side effects']])]],
['Levels of disease and epidemic curves',[
'**Sporadic** disease occurs occasionally and irregularly. **Endemic** disease is the usual, constant presence in a population (**hyperendemic** means persistently high levels). An **epidemic** is an increase in cases above what is normally expected; **outbreak** usually means the same thing in a more limited area. A **cluster** is a group of cases close in time and place that may or may not be more than expected. A **pandemic** is an epidemic that spreads over several countries or continents, affecting many people.',
'An **epidemic curve** plots cases by date of onset. A **point-source** outbreak (one exposure, such as a contaminated meal) produces a sharp rise and fall within about one **incubation period**. A **continuous common-source** outbreak (such as a contaminated water supply) produces a plateau. A **propagated** outbreak (person-to-person spread, such as measles) produces a series of progressively taller peaks about one incubation period apart.',
match('Epidemic curve and outbreak type',[['Single sharp peak','Point source'],['Plateau','Continuous common source'],['Successive peaks one incubation period apart','Propagated'],['Usual level of disease','Endemic'],['Worldwide spread','Pandemic'],['More cases than expected','Epidemic']])]],
['Steps of an outbreak investigation',[
'CDC describes these steps, which often overlap: **(1)** prepare for field work; **(2)** establish the existence of an outbreak (compare to expected numbers); **(3)** verify the diagnosis (laboratory testing, clinical review); **(4)** construct a working **case definition**; **(5)** find cases systematically and record information (a **line list**); **(6)** perform **descriptive epidemiology** (person, place, time and the epidemic curve); **(7)** develop **hypotheses**; **(8)** evaluate hypotheses epidemiologically (cohort or case-control studies); **(9)** reconsider, refine and carry out additional studies as needed (environmental, laboratory); **(10)** implement **control and prevention measures**; **(11)** initiate or maintain **surveillance**; and **(12)** **communicate** findings.',
'Control measures should begin **as soon as possible**—you do not wait for the final study if a likely source is known. A recall, closing a restaurant, vaccinating contacts or issuing a boil-water advisory can stop new cases while the investigation continues.',
order('Outbreak investigation (selected steps)',['Prepare for field work','Establish the existence of an outbreak','Verify the diagnosis','Construct a case definition','Find cases and build a line list','Describe by person, place and time','Develop and test hypotheses','Implement control measures and communicate'])]],
['Case definitions and line lists',[
'A **case definition** is a standard set of criteria for deciding whether a person has the disease being investigated. It includes **clinical criteria** (signs and symptoms) and limits of **person, place and time** (for example, “a person who attended the invented spring banquet on April 4 and developed diarrhea within 72 hours”). Cases may be classified as **confirmed** (laboratory evidence), **probable** (typical clinical features without lab confirmation) or **possible/suspected**.',
'A broad (sensitive) definition finds more cases early; a narrow (specific) definition is used later to test hypotheses. A **line list** is a table with one row per case and columns for identifiers, demographics, onset date and time, symptoms, lab results and exposures. It makes patterns visible and feeds the epidemic curve.',
chk('Which element must a case definition include?',['Clinical criteria with limits of person, place and time','The final cause of the outbreak','Only laboratory results','The name of the restaurant owner'],'Case definitions standardize who counts as a case.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('After the banquet',{a:{text:'Forty people develop vomiting and diarrhea 24–36 hours after an invented school banquet; the epidemic curve has one sharp peak. (Scenario invented for practice.) What type of outbreak is this?',choices:[['Point-source outbreak','b','Correct. One exposure, one peak.',true],['Propagated outbreak','b','Propagated outbreaks have successive peaks.',false]]},b:{text:'You list everyone who attended and ask what they ate. Which design is this?',choices:[['Retrospective cohort study','c','Correct. Compare attack rates by food.',true],['Randomized controlled trial','c','No one was assigned foods.',false]]},c:{text:'The attack rate is far higher among those who ate the rice dish. You recommend food-safety changes and keep surveillance going.',end:true}})]],
["Evaluating surveillance systems",["CDC guidelines evaluate surveillance systems with attributes such as **simplicity**, **flexibility**, **data quality**, **acceptability** (willingness of people to participate), **sensitivity** (the proportion of true cases detected), **predictive value positive** (the proportion of reported cases that are true cases), **representativeness** (whether reports reflect the population), **timeliness** and **stability**. A system can be improved by electronic laboratory reporting, clear case definitions and feedback to reporters.","Surveillance data are often described as the “tip of the iceberg.” For foodborne illness, only a small fraction of people become ill enough to see a doctor, get tested and have the result reported. This **reporting pyramid** explains why surveillance counts underestimate the true burden and why estimates use multipliers.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Foodborne illness reporting pyramid (bottom to top)","steps":["Person becomes ill","Seeks medical care","Specimen is collected","Laboratory tests and confirms","Case is reported to the health department"]},"Each step loses cases, so the reported number is only a fraction of the true total."]]
],takeaways:['Surveillance is ongoing, systematic collection, analysis, interpretation and dissemination of data—information for action; types include passive, active, sentinel, syndromic and laboratory-based (PulseNet).','NNDSS collects notifiable disease reports; other systems include NVSS, BRFSS, YRBSS, NHANES, NHSN and VAERS; levels of disease: sporadic, endemic, epidemic or outbreak, pandemic; point-source, continuous and propagated epidemic curves.','Outbreak steps: prepare, establish the outbreak, verify the diagnosis, case definition, find cases (line list), descriptive epidemiology, hypotheses, test them, refine, control measures, surveillance, communicate; case definitions include clinical criteria and person, place and time.'],terms:[['Surveillance','Ongoing systematic collection, analysis and dissemination of health data for action.'],['Case definition','Standard criteria for deciding who counts as a case.'],['Epidemic curve','A graph of cases by date of illness onset.']],mcqs:[q('Health departments calling hospitals each week to find cases is:','Active surveillance','Passive surveillance','Sentinel surveillance','Syndromic surveillance','More complete.'),q('An epidemic curve with one sharp peak suggests a:','Point-source outbreak','Propagated outbreak','Pandemic','Endemic disease','Single exposure.'),q('The constant presence of a disease in a population is:','Endemic','Epidemic','Pandemic','Sporadic','Usual level.'),q('Which CDC system compares bacterial DNA fingerprints to link cases?','PulseNet','VAERS','BRFSS','NHANES','Laboratory-based surveillance.'),q('A case definition includes clinical criteria and limits of:','Person, place and time','Cost only','Treatment','Hospital size','Standard criteria.'),q('Which step comes first in an outbreak investigation?','Prepare for field work','Implement control measures','Communicate findings','Test hypotheses','Then establish the outbreak.'),q('A table with one row per case is a:','Line list','Epidemic curve','2 × 2 table','Case definition','Organizes data.'),q('Which survey includes physical exams and lab tests?','NHANES','BRFSS','YRBSS','NNDSS','National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the steps of an outbreak investigation and explain how a case definition and an epidemic curve help investigators.',rubric:[['Early steps',['prepare','establish','existence','expected','verify','diagnosis','laboratory']],['Case definition and case finding',['case definition','clinical','person','place','time','confirmed','probable','line list']],['Analysis',['descriptive','epidemic curve','point source','propagated','hypothesis','cohort','case-control']],['Action',['control','prevention','as soon as','surveillance','communicate']]],model:'Investigators first prepare for field work by assembling a team, supplies and contacts, then establish that an outbreak exists by comparing the number of cases with what is normally expected and verify the diagnosis through laboratory testing and clinical review. They construct a working case definition that includes clinical criteria—such as vomiting or diarrhea—along with limits of person, place and time, and classify cases as confirmed, probable or possible. Using that definition, they find cases systematically and record them on a line list, one row per person with onset times, symptoms and exposures. Descriptive epidemiology follows: describing cases by person, place and time and drawing an epidemic curve, which plots cases by onset date. A single sharp peak within one incubation period points to a point-source exposure, a plateau suggests a continuing common source and successive peaks suggest person-to-person spread. These patterns help investigators develop hypotheses, which they test with analytic studies such as a retrospective cohort of everyone at an event or a case-control study, and refine with additional environmental or laboratory studies. They implement control and prevention measures as soon as a likely source is identified—even before the investigation ends—maintain surveillance to make sure the outbreak is over and communicate their findings to the public and in reports such as the MMWR.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_OUTBREAK,NNDSS,CDC_PRIN]};
